  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Suppose $(a,b,c)$ is a primitive Pythagorean triple. Prove that\n$$\\frac{(c-a)(c-b)}{2}$$\nis always a perfect square.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{(c-a)(c-b)}{2}=[n(m-n)]^2$.\n\nSubstitute Euclid’s formula and factor.",
      "worked": "By Euclid’s formula, the legs may be written as $a=m^2-n^2$, $b=2mn$ and the hypotenuse as $c=m^2+n^2$, with positive integers $m>n$. Interchanging the legs leaves the required expression unchanged.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{(c-a)(c-b)}{2}&=\\frac{\\bigl(m^2+n^2-(m^2-n^2)\\bigr)\\bigl(m^2+n^2-2mn\\bigr)}{2}\\\\\\frac{(c-a)(c-b)}{2}&=\\frac{2n^2(m-n)^2}{2}\\\\\\frac{(c-a)(c-b)}{2}&=n^2(m-n)^2\\\\\\frac{(c-a)(c-b)}{2}&=[n(m-n)]^2\\end{align*}$$\nSince $n(m-n)$ is a positive integer, the expression is a perfect square.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 72
  },
